Order of stability of alkyl substituted alkenes:

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{R}_2 \mathrm{C}=\mathrm{CR}_2>\mathrm{R}_2 \mathrm{C}=\mathrm{CHR}>\mathrm{R}_2 \mathrm{C}=\mathrm{CH}_2, \\
& \mathrm{RCH}=\mathrm{CHR}>\mathrm{RCH}=\mathrm{CH}_2>\mathrm{CH}_2=\mathrm{CH}_2 .
\end{aligned}

Greater is the number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms, greater is the stability of alkene.

\begin{gathered}
\mathrm{CH}_3-\mathrm{Br}+\mathrm{CH}_3-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{Br} \underset{\text { dry ether }}{\longrightarrow} \mathrm{CH}_3-\mathrm{CH}_3 \text { ethane } \\
\qquad \mathrm{CH}_3-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_3 \text { n-hexan } \\
\mathrm{CH}_3-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_2-\mathrm{CH}_3 \text { n-butane }
\end{gathered}

Thus, propane is not formed.

